B4 or B7. \n\nHmm. Maybe the safest is to discard W9, which is a terminal and not part of any chow. \n\nSo the answer would be to play W9. That way, I remove a terminal tile that's not part of any potential chow, and it's less likely to be useful to others. \n\nAnother check: if I discard W9, does anyone have a pung of W9? The unshown W9 is 3, so three left. The previous player has a pung of W3, but that's different. \n\nYes, so playing W9 seems safe.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for multiple chows in the T and W suits. Discarding W9 (a terminal tile not part of any planned chow) minimizes risk of aiding opponents and preserves key tiles for forming chows.\nReason: W9 is a terminal tile and not part of any sequential chow in the current hand. Discarding it avoids giving others a chance to form melds while preserving the possibility of forming multiple chows in T and W suits.\nAnswer: Play W9"}
